Title: Christmas at Vance Hall
Order: 6
Production Code: 1x06

INT. VANCE ESTATE - DRAWING ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: holly, parcels and baskets strewn about; a box of costumes just arrived from the costumer's

Molly Vance presides over Christmas preparations; Charlie Vance lounges through all of it, surrounded by pretty girls, Cora Scott foremost, who aids and abets every piece of his mischief. Talk turns, as it always does around Reuel Briggs, to his gift for mesmerism.

CHARLIE VANCE
Give the company a specimen of your infernal art, Briggs. He can raise the dead, girls, I promise you.

AUBREY LIVINGSTON
(low)
Let up, Charlie. It's no joke.

To smooth it over, Aubrey offers a story of his own.

AUBREY LIVINGSTON (CONT'D)
My father was Dr. Aubrey Livingston too — owned a plantation, and a slave girl among the house servants named Mira, who he'd throw into a trance for the amusement of guests. One dinner, two years before the war, he called her out to perform. Instead of tricks, she told the company what she saw — the women all widows, the men in early graves, "a down-trodden race" ruling the land, and for him, a prison cell and a pauper's grave. The dinner party broke up in a panic. My own father died a prisoner of war in Boston Harbor, and every woman at that table was left a widow. He sold her a few months before the South seceded. Never heard of her again.

MR. VANCE
(with the others)
Wonderful!

AUBREY LIVINGSTON
There is only too much truth in the science of mesmeric phenomena. The world is a wonderful place.

Then, thinking of the friendless patient in Reuel's care, he changes the subject, and Molly takes it up.

MOLLY VANCE
Tomorrow after lunch we'll carry the patients fruit and flowers. And as for your beautiful patient, Dr. Briggs — she shall not be friendless any longer. She shall come to us at Vance Hall.

She will not be argued out of it. In the months that follow, Dianthe Lusk — given the name "Felice Adams" — will come to live under the Vance roof.
